---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/TableOfContents.astro';
import BitPicture from '../components/BitPicture.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="Understand the machine and use that understanding to make things. Code198x teaches programming and game development through vintage computers.">
  <article class="about">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">About Code198x</p>
      <h1>Understand the machine.<br />Make something with it.</h1>
      <p class="lead">A game begins with an idea. Making it work takes us into rules,
        pictures, sound and the program that brings them together. Code198x follows
        those connections all the way down to the machine.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="reading-layout">
    <div class="reading-body">
    <section aria-labelledby="purpose">
      <h2 id="purpose">Why we’re building this</h2>
      <p>Computers can feel like sealed boxes. We want readers to be able to open them
        up intellectually: to understand what a variable represents, how a loop runs,
        why a picture takes memory and what happens between pressing a key and seeing
        a response.</p>
      <p>Vintage machines offer a manageable place to investigate. Their constraints
        make choices visible. We can follow a small program, examine its data and
        change something to find out whether our explanation holds.</p>
      <p>That understanding gives us more freedom to create. It also helps when we
        return to modern tools: we can ask what an abstraction does for us, what it
        costs and when we need to look underneath it.</p>
    </section>

    <BitPicture />

    <section aria-labelledby="learning">
      <h2 id="learning">Learn by making and investigating</h2>
      <p>We are developing a games development and programming curriculum for children,
        parents, teachers and anyone curious about computing. You do not need to have
        owned an old computer, or to know its language before you begin.</p>
      <p>Each game grows through guided, runnable stages. We explain the immediate
        programming and design choices, then connect them to fuller explanations.
        Small games have a place here: a good puzzle or a single room can give us
        something worthwhile to build, play and improve.</p>
      <p><a href="/foundations">Foundations</a> explores programming with pseudocode.{' '}
        <a href="/craft">The Craft</a> brings together game-making ideas, including{' '}
        <a href="/craft/maths-for-games">Maths for Games</a> and <a href="/craft/game-feel">Game Feel</a>. System tracks put ideas to work in a particular language
        and machine. These shared subjects support your chosen route; they are not
        courses you must finish before making a game.</p>
      <p>BASIC and assembly offer different ways in. <a href="/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/assembly">Spectrum assembly</a> does not require
        completing <a href="/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/basic">Spectrum BASIC</a>. Across the curriculum, we choose languages and
        projects to suit the machine, and explain what transfers when you try another.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="systems">
      <h2 id="systems">Different machines, different possibilities</h2>
      <p>The first focus is the <a href="/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum">ZX Spectrum</a>, <a href="/systems/commodore-64">Commodore 64</a>, <a href="/systems/commodore-amiga">Commodore Amiga</a> and <a href="/systems/nintendo-entertainment-system">NES</a>.
        The wider curriculum will explore other computers and consoles through the
        things they make possible and the choices behind their design.</p>
      <p>Original hardware is a useful starting point. Expansions and successor machines
        can offer further subjects to investigate. When a project needs extra hardware,
        we will say what it provides and what the original machine still does.
        Modern development tools do not change the capabilities of the target.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/systems">systems directory</a> distinguishes current lessons from
        planned coverage. The curriculum is growing; a system page does not mean its
        teaching material is complete.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="connections">
      <h2 id="connections">Follow the connections</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/vault">Vault</a> is our encyclopaedia of computing: machines,
        games, people and the circumstances in which they worked. The{' '}
        <a href="/patterns">Pattern Library</a> collects practical techniques to adapt
        to your own projects, with their requirements and trade-offs.</p>
      <p><a href="/from-the-metal">From the Metal</a> examines programming through
        concrete machine behaviour. <a href="/field-notes">Field Notes</a> tells the
        stories behind our investigations. <a href="/updates">What’s New</a> records
        useful additions and changes so you can find what has become available.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="trust">
      <h2 id="trust">Work readers can question</h2>
      <p>We aim to make claims checkable. Historical accounts need sources; code needs
        more than a successful build. We distinguish what has been proposed, built or
        observed running, including the machine or emulator used to check it.</p>
      <p>AI assists with development and drafting. Its output needs review and evidence,
        just as other contributions do. Our <a href="/standards">Sources &amp; Standards</a>{' '}
        explain the public bar. Corrections and questions help us improve both the
        material and our understanding.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="people">
      <h2 id="people">Who’s behind it</h2>
      <p>I’m Steve Hill. Early experiences typing programs with my dad and experimenting
        on a Spectrum left me wanting to make computers do things. That curiosity is
        why I’m building Code198x.</p>
      <p>The lessons are free to read, with original prose licensed under{}
        <a href="/standards#licence">CC BY 4.0</a>. The project is developed in the open. If you
        know a machine well, can help explain a subject or have worked through a lesson
        and found a problem, I’d like to hear from you.</p>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/orgs/code198x/discussions">Join the discussion</a>,
        explore <a href="/contribute">ways to contribute</a>, or{' '}
        <a href="/start-here">find a place to start learning</a>.</p>
    </section>
    </div>
    <aside class="reading-toc"><TableOfContents selector=".reading-body" /></aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 240px; gap: var(--space-12); align-items: start; }
  .reading-body { min-width: 0; }
  .reading-body > section:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .reading-toc { position: sticky; top: 7rem; }
  header p, section p { max-width: 70ch; }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) { .reading-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .reading-toc { display: none; } }

  .about { max-width: var(--container-max-width); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--space-10) var(--gutter) var(--space-16); }
  header { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .eyebrow { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); font-size: var(--h-text-small); color: var(--h-ink-muted); }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); line-height: 1.08; color: var(--h-ink); margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  section { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  h2 { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--h-ink); }
  p { font-family: var(--font-family-read); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--h-ink-body); margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .15em; }
  a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
</style>
